    // weight fell with it - a term that faded the wire away to cover the body the flat strap did not have, a strap
    // having no top to show from overhead. The wire's light came from straight up besides (n = vec3(0., 1., 0.)), off
    // no face at all. Both are gone with the band. The tube has the body, and nothing is faded now for want of one.
    // THE LIGHT IS THE TUBE'S OWN: each face the lens sees takes the sun by its own normal (fw_light, whose expression
    // is the same as the last line of this shader gives every other face of what stands on the land - the same light
    // written out twice and KEPT IN STEP BY HAND, not shared, so that a round about a wire does not touch the light of
    // every flat face in this world: the owner's ruling, and the world file's list of what is held true by hand), in
    // the share of the breadth it shows across the sight line.
    // ON THIS FLIGHT ONLY ONE OF THE TWO WAYS BELOW IS EVER TAKEN. The tube is finer than a pixel at every metre of the
    // lens's line, so the light is ALWAYS the mean of the faces the lens sees, weighted by the breadth each shows, and
    // the sleeve carries every fragment of the wire: the tubes are 72 of the fence's 144 triangles and they put no
    // pixel on the screen on this line (their share is band times wown, and wown is nought at every metre of it). The
    // tube's own faces begin to carry the wire only nearer than 10 to 11 m, and carry it alone inside 3 to 4 m (wseen
    // over wfoot of 1 and of 3, at the tube's 2.6 to 3.0 cm and 374.4 pixels to the tangent at 800 by 450); the line
    // passes 14.2 m from the nearest wire at its nearest, at metre 236.9 of 455.1 (u 0.521), measured on the engine's
    // own path. So EACH VISIBLE FACE'S OWN LIGHT, in the share of the breadth it shows, IS WHAT EVERY WIRE FRAGMENT OF
    // THIS FLIGHT IS GIVEN, since wown is nought at every metre of it; what is true of this code and NOT REACHED ON THIS
    // FLIGHT is the other term alone, "the face the pixel lies on carries it alone", for which one must stand nearer
    // than the film goes.
    // AS THE LENS'S OWN WEIGHTS VANISH THE LIGHT SETTLES ON THE TUBE'S PLAIN MEAN. With the sight line swung onto the
    // wire's own line every face's share of the breadth (wf) goes to nothing together, and the plain quotient
    // wmean / max(wshow, 1e-4) fell through that floor TO BLACK - a go-to-nothing of just the kind this round mends.
    // So the quotient carries a settle: e times the plain mean of the six faces in the numerator and e in the
    // denominator, e = 1e-4. Where wshow is anything at all the pull is e / wshow and cannot be seen: the least wshow
    // anywhere on the lens's line is 0.114 (metre 230, where a sight line comes within 3.7 degrees of a wire's own
    // line), so the pull there is one part in 1137 of the colour, and one step of the picture's 8 bits is some 5 parts
    // in 100 of a colour this dark - a fiftieth of a step. Counted in those steps the old line and the new are the same
    // number from square across the wire down to a tenth of a degree off its line, one step apart at a hundredth of a
    // degree, and where the old gave 0,0,0 the new holds the plain mean (49,42,36 after the tone, by a plain gamma).
    // WHY THAT SIZE: e must stand well over the float32 noise of the six dots where they cancel (3e-8 at a millionth of
    // a radian off the line) and well under the least honest wshow (0.114); 1e-4 is a thousand times the one and more
    // than a thousand under the other, and it is the floor the quotient already had, now given a body to settle on.
    // TWO MORE FLOORS STAND IN THIS CHAIN, named so that the list is whole: wfoot's own 1e-5 here, and band's inner one
    // of 1e-4 where it is called. NEITHER CAN DRIVE WHAT IS DRAWN TOWARD NOTHING: they can only hold it up, though
    // raising a footprint also softens a bar's edge, which can lower the pixel at the very edge while raising its
    // neighbour outside. Neither is reachable on this flight, where wfoot is never less than 14.2 over 374.4, 3.8 cm.
    // WHAT IS DRAWN KEEPS THAT SHAPE STILL, AND IT IS NAMED HERE UNMENDED: wseen is the half breadth times wshow over
    // wsin, and wsin has a floor of 1e-4 where wshow has none, so with the sight line swung within about a
    // TEN-THOUSANDTH of a radian of the wire's own line (the floor's own 1e-4, which is 0.0057 of a degree) the quotient
